  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Consider us underwhelmed by the overall progression \nof things these past 24 hours. Synoptically there has not been much \nchange in the setup with several boxes already checked for the \npotential for heavy rainfall. This includes a deep warm cloud \nlayer, ample moisture throughout the atmospheric column, and \nPWATs of greater than 2\" which would typically point towards a \nheavy rain event across ENC. However, the continued trend \ndepicting a lack of organized surface convergence/boundaries \nsuggests more scattered activity which will be more progressive\nin nature thus lowering the heavy rainfall threat. As a result \nhave cancelled the flood watch that was out for portions of ENC.\nWhile their will be periods of torrential downpours and heavy \nrainfall think the flooding risk has continued to trend down \ntoday and will be more isolated in nature. \n\nAs mentioned above there is little change in the synoptic setup as \nan upper level trough currently in the Tennessee River Valley has \nstalled across the area and will remain there into midweek before \nweakening and eventually getting absorbed by a passing northern \nstream trough Wed/Thurs. This should keep the moisture channel open \nfrom the Gulf into at least midweek as upper level S-SW'rly winds \nremain in place advecting moisture N'wards. This second trough \nshould remain well enough to the north to preclude any impacts to \nthe area later this week. At the same time we still expect a few \nshortwaves to transit around the base of this trough and lift north \nthrough the Carolinas this afternoon into tonight, Tues and \npotentially again on Wed. As the upper trough dissipates/gets \nabsorbed later this week any mid level forcing does look to shift \nfurther inland away from ENC. \n\nWhat does this mean for us as far as ambient weather goes through \nWed? Stalled surface front to our west will move little before \ndissipating on Wed. Ongoing isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are expected this continue this afternoon with \nperiods of even lower coverage this evening before the next \nshortwave transits across the area tonight into Tue morning \nbringing a renewed threat for more widespread shower and \nthunderstorm activity. Given the trends, expect widespread \nrainfall amounts around 0.5-1.5\" across ENC over the next 48 \nhours. REFS and HREF probs for greater than 2\" of rainfall over \na 48 hour period continue to go on the downtrend with chances \nnow closer to 30-50%, thus lending credence to a lower flooding \nrisk. While the flooding risk is lower than previously \nanticipated, that does not mean it is zero as any shower or \nstorm will have a chance to produce rainfall rates of at least \n1-2\" an hour which could yield an isolated and localized threat\nfor flooding in slower moving storms. Areas of concern appear \nto be along the Crystal Coast and along the OBX south of Oregon \nInlet if this did occur. \n\nWe are now in a Marginal (level 1/4) Excessive Rain Outlook for\ntoday and tomorrow. \n\nBy the end of the week it looks like we return back to climo or \nbelow climo PoPs as a lack of mid level shortwaves or significant \nforcing will be noted across ENC. This will likely result in chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms along the daily seabreeze but once \nagain these chances should be isolated at best given the overall \nsetup. After a cool start to the week given widespread cloudcover \nand rain as highs only get into the low to mid 80s and lows in the \nlow 70s through Wed, we do begin to warm back up with temps \nreturning to the upper 80s to low 90s inland and mid 80s along the \nOBX.",
